// Star catalog: the article for each object (Tom, 2026-11-24).
// Our own words; each fact checked against the Wikipedia article in facts.js (wiki).
// text: paragraphs split by a blank line. photo: { src, alt, credit }, once Tom approves photos.
// photos: inline pictures, each { src, alt, credit, after }, after the paragraph at index after (Tom, 2026-11-24).

export const ARTICLES = {
  zetaoph: { text: "Zeta Ophiuchi is a hot blue star in the constellation Ophiuchus. It lies roughly 400 to 440 light years from Earth. No O-type star, the class of very hot blue stars, lies closer to our Solar System. From Earth it shines at magnitude 2.6. Only two stars in its constellation look brighter.\n\nIt is heavy, at about 19 to 20 M☉, and about eight times as wide as the Sun. Its surface is near 34,300 K. It spins very fast, perhaps once a day, which is close to the speed that would pull it apart. The fast spin leaves the poles hotter than the middle: about 39,000 K at the poles and 30,700 K at the equator.\n\nDust in space between here and the star soaks up much of its light, most of all the blue light. Without that dust, Zeta Ophiuchi would reach magnitude 1.54 and rank with the brightest stars we can see.\n\nZeta Ophiuchi is a runaway star. It rushes through space at about 30 km each second. It probably once had a heavier partner. When that partner blew up in a supernova, a huge star explosion, Zeta Ophiuchi was flung away. A pulsar, PSR B1929+10, could be what is left of that partner. As the star pushes through thick dust, it piles up a curved wall of gas ahead of it, called a bow shock. NASA’s WISE telescope pictured it in infrared light.\n\nIn 1979, astronomers saw odd moving bumps in the star’s light when they split it into colours. Stars with the same pattern are now called Zeta Oph stars. The star is young, aged six million years or a bit more, and about halfway through the first stage of its life. In a few million years it will grow into a red supergiant so big it would reach past Jupiter’s orbit. Then it will explode and leave a neutron star behind.", photos: [{"src": "photos/zetaoph.webp", "alt": "Infrared image from the Spitzer Space Telescope of Zeta Ophiuchi, a bright star with a curved bow shock of glowing dust in front of it.", "credit": "NASA/JPL-Caltech / Spitzer, public domain", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/zetaoph-toy.webp", "alt": "Zeta Ophiuchi as this toy draws it", "credit": "Zeta Ophiuchi in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  regulus: { text: "Regulus shines brighter than any other star in Leo, the Lion, and it ranks among the brightest points of light in our sky. It lies about 79 light years away. Its name is Latin for \"little king\" or \"prince\". Regulus sits less than half a degree from the ecliptic, the line the Sun, Moon and planets follow across the sky, so the Moon often slides in front of it.\n\nTo the eye it looks like one star, but there are four, in two pairs. The main star, Regulus A, is blue-white and about 4.15 times as massive as the Sun. It spins so fast that one turn takes only 15.9 hours, while the Sun needs about 25 days. Its equator races along at about 320 km per second, close to the speed that would tear it apart. The spin squashes it into a flattened shape, so the poles, at about 14,500 K, are hotter and brighter than the middle, at about 11,000 K.\n\nA small, faint partner circles Regulus A in about 40 days. It weighs at least 0.3 times as much as the Sun and is most likely a white dwarf, the cooling core of a dead star. Far out, about 5,000 AU away, a second pair, an orange dwarf and a red dwarf, go round each other in roughly 600 years.\n\nPeople have watched this star for thousands of years. To the Babylonians it was Sharru, \"the King\", and the Arabic name Qalb al-Asad means \"heart of the lion\". Astronomers once thought Regulus A was only 50 to 100 million years old. A white dwarf partner means the system must be a billion years old or more, perhaps because Regulus A once took gas from its partner and grew.\n\nPlanets rarely cover it. Venus last passed in front of Regulus on 7 July 1959 and will do so again on 1 October 2044.", photos: [{"src": "photos/regulus.webp", "alt": "Regulus as a bright point of light, photographed through an amateur telescope.", "credit": "Drew Farwell, CC BY-SA 3.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/regulus-toy.webp", "alt": "Regulus as this toy draws it", "credit": "Regulus in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  vega: { text: "Vega is the brightest star in Lyra, the Lyre, and the fifth brightest star in the night sky. It lies about 25 light years away, close by the measure of the galaxy. With Deneb and Altair it makes the Summer Triangle, a large shape that is easy to find on summer evenings in the north.\n\nIt is a young white star, about twice as heavy as the Sun and some 700 million years old. A heavier star burns its fuel faster, so Vega will live only about a tenth as long as the Sun. It also holds fewer heavy elements than the Sun does.\n\nVega spins fast, once in about 16 hours. The spin pushes its middle outward, so the star is wider at its equator than from pole to pole. Its poles are the hottest part, about 10,000 K, and its equator is cooler, about 8,900 K. From the Earth we look almost straight down onto one pole, so to us it looks round and even.\n\nVega is a star of firsts. In July 1850 it became the first star after the Sun to be photographed, with a long exposure at Harvard. In 1872 Henry Draper photographed its spectrum, the first picture of a star's light to show dark lines. For a long time astronomers used Vega to set the zero of the brightness scale.\n\nThe Earth's axis slowly wobbles, so the pole star changes over thousands of years. Around 12,000 BCE Vega stood close to the north celestial pole, and it will come near the pole again in about 12,000 years.\n\nIn 1983 the IRAS satellite found more infrared light from Vega than a star alone gives. The extra light comes from a wide disc of dust round it, made as rocks collide, like the Sun's Kuiper belt. Stars with such discs are now called Vega-like. In 2024 the Webb and Hubble telescopes showed the disc to be very smooth, with no clear sign of large planets shaping it.", photos: [{src: "photos/vega-toy.webp", alt: "Vega's limb as this toy draws it, a pale blue disc with a soft corona", credit: "Vega in this toy", after: 2}] },
  sirius: { text: "No other star at night looks as bright as Sirius. It shines in Canis Major, the Greater Dog, a southern constellation next to Orion. It is only about 8.6 light years away, one of our closest neighbours among the stars. Its name comes from an old Greek word that means glowing or scorching.\n\nSirius is really two stars. The bright one, Sirius A, is a white star with about twice the Sun’s mass, and it gives out about 25 times as much light as the Sun. Its partner, Sirius B, is a white dwarf: the small, hot core that is left when a star dies. Sirius B is about as wide as the Earth, yet it holds about as much mass as the Sun. The two go around each other once every 50 years.\n\nLong ago, Sirius B was the heavier of the pair. It burned through its fuel, swelled into a red giant, threw off its outer layers and shrank. That happened some 120 million years ago, and the system as a whole is 200 to 300 million years old.\n\nIn Ancient Egypt, the first dawn rising of Sirius each year told people that the Nile flood was coming. Its nickname, the Dog Star, gave us the phrase dog days for the hottest part of summer. In 1844 Friedrich Bessel saw that its path across the sky wobbled, and he worked out that a hidden partner must pull on it. Alvan Graham Clark first saw Sirius B in 1862.\n\nSirius is slowly getting closer to us, so it will grow a little brighter during the coming 60,000 years. In about the year 66,270 it will act as a pole star for the south. It should stay the brightest star in our sky for about 210,000 years, until Vega takes over. Sirius A will one day run out of hydrogen in its core and end as a white dwarf too.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sirius.webp", "alt": "A Hubble picture of Sirius A as a bright blue-white glare with light spikes, and its partner Sirius B as a tiny dot to the lower left.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, H. Bond and M. Barstow / Hubble, public domain", "after": 1}, {"src": "photos/sirius-toy.webp", "alt": "Sirius as this toy draws it", "credit": "Sirius in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  procyon: { text: "About 11.46 ly away, this white star is one of our nearest neighbours and in most years ranks eighth among the brightest stars of the night sky. A faint white dwarf, Procyon B, circles it every 40.84 years. Bessel suspected that partner in 1844 from the bright star’s motion, yet nobody saw it until 1896. Between 10 and 100 million years from now the main star should swell to between 80 and 150 times its present width." },
  sun: { text: "Our Sun is the star at the heart of the Solar System. This giant ball of plasma, a hot glowing gas, shines because hydrogen fuses into helium deep inside. It is about 150 million km from Earth, so sunlight needs about 8 minutes to get here.\n\nNothing else in the Solar System comes close to it in size or weight. It is about 1.39 million km across, roughly 109 times as wide as Earth, and holds about 99.86% of the Solar System’s mass. The surface is about 5,800 K and the core about 15.7 million K. The thin outer layer, the corona, is far hotter than the surface, at 1 to 2 million K.\n\nIt is a G2 V star, sometimes called a yellow dwarf. Its light is really white; it only looks yellow through our air. The Sun does not spin as one solid body: its equator turns about once every 25 days, but near the poles a turn takes about 33 days. Sunspots and flares come and go in a cycle of about 11 years.\n\nIt took shape some 4.6 billion years ago, when part of a huge cloud of dust and gas collapsed under its own weight. Most of it became the Sun, and the rest formed the planets. People have honoured the Sun as a god and studied it since ancient times. In 1868 Norman Lockyer found a new element in its light and named it helium, after Helios, the Greek Sun god.\n\nThe Sun is roughly halfway through its main life. In around 5 billion years its core will run out of hydrogen, and it will swell into a red giant, swallowing Mercury and Venus and perhaps Earth. Later it will throw off its outer gas as a glowing cloud called a planetary nebula. What is left will be a white dwarf, a dense core that slowly cools for trillions of years.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sun.webp", "alt": "The Sun photographed in white light through a solar filter on 8 May 2019, with two small groups of dark sunspots and a darker edge.", "credit": "Matúš Motlo, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/sun-toy.webp", "alt": "The Sun as this toy draws it", "credit": "The Sun in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  epseri: { text: "Epsilon Eridani is an orange star in Eridanus, the River, just below the celestial equator, so people on most of Earth can see it. It lies about 10.5 light years away. Of all the stars and star systems we can see without a telescope, only two are closer. Since 2015 it has also had a proper name, Ran, after a Norse goddess of the sea.\n\nIt is a little smaller and lighter than the Sun, with about 0.82 times the Sun’s mass and about three quarters of its width. It gives off about a third as much light, and its surface is near 5,000 K. It is also young: most estimates put its age between about 200 million and 1 billion years. Young stars are restless. It has strong magnetic activity and dark spots, and its wind of particles blows 30 times harder than the Sun’s. It turns once in about 11 days.\n\nAstronomers think a giant planet goes round it every 7.3 to 7.6 years. The planet, now called AEgir, was announced in 2000 and was argued about for nearly two decades before most experts accepted it. Rings of dust circle the star too: a cold belt of comets about 70 au out, like our Kuiper belt, and warmer dust from about 3 to 20 au. The empty gap between them hints at more planets.\n\nPtolemy listed the star in his catalogue in the 2nd century AD. Between 1881 and 1883 William Elkin measured how far away it is. Frank Drake’s Project Ozma listened to it for radio signals from aliens, but heard nothing. A second try in 2010 found nothing either.\n\nIn about 31,500 years a nearby pair of stars, Luyten 726-8, will pass within about 0.93 light years of it. Writers of science fiction love it, and some studies have named it as a goal for travel between the stars.", photos: [{"src": "photos/epseri.webp", "alt": "A radio telescope image of Epsilon Eridani as a dot at the centre of a wide, oval ring of cold dust.", "credit": "Mark Booth / ALMA, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}, {"src": "photos/epseri-toy.webp", "alt": "Epsilon Eridani as this toy draws it", "credit": "Epsilon Eridani in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  proxima: { text: "Apart from the Sun, Proxima Centauri is the star nearest to us. It lies about 4.25 light years away, in Centaurus, a constellation of the southern sky. Its name is Latin and means the nearest star of Centaurus. It belongs to the Alpha Centauri family of stars, so it is also called Alpha Centauri C.\n\nProxima is a red dwarf, a small and cool star. It has only about one eighth of the Sun’s mass and is about one seventh as wide. It is packed tight, though: on average it is about 33 times denser than the Sun. It is far too dim to see with the eye alone. You need a telescope at least 8 cm across to spot it.\n\nIt is also a flare star. Its magnetic field now and then sets off sudden bursts that make the star much brighter for a short time. In 2016 one huge flare made it about 68 times brighter than normal. The strongest flares reach about 27 million K, which is hot enough to give off X-rays.\n\nRobert Innes, a Scottish astronomer working in South Africa, found the star in 1915. He noticed that it moved across the sky in step with Alpha Centauri. Today we know of two planets around Proxima, and a third that is still in doubt. One of them, Proxima b, lies in the zone where liquid water could exist, and it goes around the star in about 11 days. Whether life could survive the flares there is still unclear.\n\nProxima has held the title of nearest star for some 32,000 years and should keep it for about 25,000 more. Small red dwarfs burn their fuel slowly, so it will keep shining for about four trillion more years. It will never swell into a red giant. Instead it will slowly become a white dwarf made mostly of helium, a dim leftover core that cools over time.", photos: [{"src": "photos/proxima.webp", "alt": "A Hubble picture of Proxima Centauri shining brightly with four light spikes, among many faint background stars.", "credit": "ESA/Hubble & NASA, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}, {"src": "photos/proxima-toy.webp", "alt": "Proxima Centauri as this toy draws it", "credit": "Proxima Centauri in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  luhman: { text: "Two brown dwarfs, objects between a star and a gas giant, circle each other just 6.5 ly from the Sun, the closest such bodies known. They hold about 35 and 29 Jupiter masses and need roughly 27 years per orbit. Though so near, they were found only in 2013, lost against the crowded star fields of the Milky Way’s plane. Fast winds comb their clouds into stripes along the equator, much like Jupiter’s." },
  wise: { text: "WISE 0855−0714 is a brown dwarf, an object too light to shine by fusion like a true star. It lies about 7.4 light years away in the constellation Hydra. Only three star systems are closer to the Sun, so it is the fourth nearest.\n\nNo colder brown dwarf is known. Its temperature is about 276 K, or 3 °C, close to room temperature. It is about as wide as Jupiter but holds only 3 to 10 times Jupiter’s mass. That is so light that it could count as a planet drifting alone in space, though research papers still class it among brown dwarfs.\n\nBecause it is so cold, it gives off almost no visible light. It glows mainly in infrared, the heat light our eyes cannot see, so infrared telescopes, like the James Webb Space Telescope and WISE, see it best. Webb found water vapour, methane, ammonia and carbon monoxide in its air. In 2026 a team found patchy clouds of water ice there.\n\nThe WISE space telescope first took its picture on 4 May 2010 while mapping the whole sky. In March 2013 Kevin Luhman noticed that it moved across the sky very fast, a sign that it is close. Only two stars, Barnard’s Star and Kapteyn’s Star, move faster across our sky. Luhman checked it with the Spitzer and Gemini North telescopes, and NASA announced the find in April 2014.\n\nIts brightness changes a little, by 4 to 5%, which suggests it turns once every 10 to 14 hours. Webb also watched it for 11 hours to look for moons passing in front of it, but found none. A moon around double the size of Titan, a moon of Saturn, would most likely have shown up.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wise.webp", "alt": "Two infrared JWST pictures taken about five months apart, in which the orange point of WISE 0855−0714 has moved against the background galaxies.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / JWST, Alves de Oliveira et al., processed by Melina Thévenot, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 3}, {"src": "photos/wise-toy.webp", "alt": "WISE 0855−0714 as this toy draws it", "credit": "WISE 0855−0714 in this toy", "after": 3}] },
  ttauri: { text: "T Tauri is a young star system in Taurus, the Bull, and it changes in brightness. From Earth it seems to sit among the Hyades, a star cluster, but it is really about 318 light years farther away. It gave its name to a whole class of baby stars, the T Tauri stars, even though it is an odd example of them.\n\nThere are three stars. The brightest, T Tau N, has about 2.1 to 2.4 times the Sun’s mass. Roughly 300 AU away is a southern pair. T Tau Sa has about 2 to 2.3 times the Sun’s mass, and T Tau Sb about 0.4 to 0.5. These two are only about 7 AU apart and circle each other in about 27 years.\n\nStars this young do not yet fuse hydrogen in their cores. They glow with heat left over from their collapse, and they still pull in gas from discs around them, so their light flickers over weeks or months. Fast jets of gas stream out. A ring of dust around the southern pair blocks its visible light, so we see that pair mainly in infrared.\n\nJohn Russell Hind found T Tauri in October 1852, together with a nearby cloud now called Hind’s Variable Nebula. That cloud fades and comes back. In 1890 Sherburne Burnham spotted a small glowing patch right next to the star. It was the first Herbig-Haro object ever found: a bright knot made where a jet crashes into cold gas nearby.\n\nThe system is still changing. Between 2015 and 2024 T Tau N faded by about 2 magnitudes, likely because the dust ring of the southern pair is moving in front of it. The dimming may go on for 60 to 70 years, perhaps longer, and the star may vanish from view in visible light. A gap in the disc around T Tau N hints at a planet about as heavy as Saturn.", photos: [{"src": "photos/ttauri.webp", "alt": "A Hubble ultraviolet image of the T Tauri system, mapping the glow of molecular hydrogen around the young stars.", "credit": "NASA/ESA Hubble, A. Brown et al., M. Thévenot, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 1}] },
  fomalhaut: { text: "No star in Piscis Austrinus, the Southern Fish, outshines Fomalhaut, and it ranks high among the brightest stars we see at night. It is about 25 light years away from us. It is best seen from the southern half of the Earth, but people in much of the north can see it too, low in the sky in autumn.\n\nFomalhaut is a young white star, about 440 million years old. It has nearly twice the Sun’s mass and is about 1.84 times as wide. It gives out about 16 times as much light as the Sun, and its surface is about 8,600 K.\n\nWhat makes Fomalhaut special is the dust around it. Several rings of dust and rock circle the star. The outer ring lies about 133 AU out, which is 133 times the distance from the Earth to the Sun, and it has a sharp inner edge. Comets that crash into each other keep it supplied with fresh dust, perhaps about 2,000 of them each day. New Scientist compared it to the Great Eye of Sauron.\n\nIn 2008 the Hubble Space Telescope took pictures of what seemed to be a planet just inside the outer ring. Named Fomalhaut b, it was the first planet candidate around another star seen in visible light. Later studies, in 2019 and 2023, found that it was growing and fading. It now looks like a spreading cloud of dust from a big crash between rocky bodies, not a planet. The James Webb Space Telescope has also looked closely at the rings.\n\nFomalhaut has two far away partners, the red dwarf LP 876-10 and the smaller star TW Piscis Austrini. They are spread over about 8 degrees of sky, yet all three move through space together. Its name comes from Arabic words for the mouth of the fish, and the Persians counted it as one of their four royal stars.", photos: [{"src": "photos/fomalhaut.webp", "alt": "A Hubble picture of the oval ring of dust around Fomalhaut, glowing orange, with the glare of the star itself blocked out at the centre.", "credit": "NASA / Hubble, public domain", "after": 2}] },
  arcturus: { text: "The brightest star of the northern sky is a red giant about 7.1 billion years old. It holds roughly one Sun’s mass, yet has swollen so far that it pours out about 170 times the Sun’s light. It races by at 122 km/s and will pass closest to us in about 4,000 years. In 1933 its light opened the Chicago World’s Fair, chosen because it was thought to have set out during the 1893 fair." },
  betalyr: { text: "Beta Lyrae, also called Sheliak, is a group of stars in the constellation Lyra. It lies about 960 light years away. Without a telescope it looks like one point of light, but it holds six stars. The bright main part is itself a close pair that eclipse each other, plus a possible third star.\n\nThe two stars of the close pair orbit each other every 12.9 days. Seen from Earth, their orbit is almost edge on, so each star passes in front of the other in turn. Because of this, the system’s brightness rises and falls between magnitude 3.2 and 4.4.\n\nThe pair are so close that they share material. The star that is now lighter was once the heavier one. It aged faster, swelled into a giant, and spilled most of its gas onto its partner. Today the gainer has about 13 times the Sun’s mass and the giver about 3. The gas still flows, enough to build a whole Sun in 50,000 years, and that makes the orbit about 19 seconds longer each year.\n\nThe gainer star hides inside a thick disk of gas, and jets stick out above and below it. The disk blocks our view of that star, so its exact type is hard to pin down. The disk itself gives about a fifth of the light of the whole system.\n\nIn 1784 John Goodricke, an amateur astronomer from Britain, found that its light changes. In 1894 Aristarkh Belopolsky showed that it is an eclipsing pair. In 2008 the CHARA Array, a group of linked telescopes, made the first picture of the giver star and the disk, and the orbit could be worked out for the first time. Beta Lyrae became the model for a whole class of eclipsing stars. It sits in a loose cluster of roughly 100 stars called Gaia 8, thought to be 30 to 100 million years old." },
  s5hvs1: { text: "S5-HVS1 is a star in Grus, the Crane, a constellation of the southern sky. It lies about 29,000 light years away. It is a main-sequence star of type A: a hot, white star that is still burning hydrogen in its core.\n\nWhat makes it special is its speed. It moves at about 1,755 km per second, almost 0.6 percent of light’s speed. When it was found in 2019, no faster star of its kind was known. At that speed the pull of the Milky Way cannot hold it, and it is on its way out of our galaxy for good.\n\nAstronomers think it was thrown out by Sagittarius A*, the giant black hole in the middle of the Milky Way. A likely story is that the star once had a partner, and the two stars came too close to the black hole. Its pull tore the pair apart, and S5-HVS1 was flung outward at huge speed. This idea is called the Hills mechanism. If it is right, S5-HVS1 is the first star known to have been ejected this way.\n\nThe star was found by Sergey Koposov of Carnegie Mellon University and his team. They were working on the Southern Stellar Stream Spectroscopic Survey, called S5 for short, which gave the star the first part of its name. HVS1 stands for hypervelocity star, a term for stars that move fast enough to escape the galaxy. It also has an older catalogue name, HE 2251-5127.\n\nIts trip links two very different places. It began deep in the crowded centre of the galaxy, right beside the black hole, and it is now out in the quiet southern sky, heading into the empty space between galaxies." },
  rigel: { text: "Rigel is a huge, hot, blue star of the kind called a supergiant. It sits in the constellation Orion, where it marks one of the hunter’s feet. Most of the time it outshines every other star in Orion, and it is the seventh brightest in the night sky. It lies about 850 light years from us.\n\nRigel is roughly 74 to 79 times as wide as the Sun and about 21 times as heavy, and its surface is about 12,100 K. Guesses of how much light it gives out vary a lot, from about 61,500 to 363,000 times the Sun’s, because its distance is hard to pin down. A strong stellar wind, a stream of gas flowing off its surface, carries matter away from it.\n\nRigel looks like one point of light, but it is a group of four or more stars. Close beside it sits a much fainter set of three blue-white stars, each with three to four times the Sun’s mass. Two of them orbit one another in 10 days, and the third circles that pair every 63 years. Rigel and the set of three take about 24,000 years to go around each other.\n\nThe name Rigel comes from Arabic words for the left foot of Jauzah, an old Arabic name for Orion. The first known written record of the name dates from 1521. For the Māori of New Zealand the star is Puanga, and its rising before dawn can signal that their new year is beginning.\n\nRigel is only about 8 million years old, yet it has already used up the hydrogen in its core and swollen into a supergiant. Its brightness changes a little as its outer layers pulse. One day it will blow up as a supernova. Seen from Earth, that blast could shine about as bright as a quarter Moon. It would leave behind a black hole, or else a neutron star.", photos: [{"src": "photos/rigel.webp", "alt": "Rigel as a bright white dot in a small telescope, with its much fainter companion just below and to the right.", "credit": "NVN271, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  betelgeuse: { text: "Put in the Sun’s place, its surface would reach past the orbit of Mars. Over the winter of 2019 it lost about two thirds of its light; Hubble traced the dimming to dust from a burst off its own surface. It will likely explode as a supernova within 100,000 years." },
  vycma: { text: "VY Canis Majoris, a red hypergiant, is among the largest stars known. It sits in Canis Major, about 3,700 to 3,900 light years from us. It also ranks with the most luminous stars of our galaxy, the Milky Way.\n\nIts radius is about 1,420 times the Sun’s. If it took the Sun’s place, it would stretch past the orbit of Jupiter. Light would need 6 hours to go once around its surface, while for the Sun it needs only 14.5 seconds. It has about 17 times the Sun’s mass, give or take 8, and recent estimates say it gives off about 180,000 to 270,000 times as much light as the Sun.\n\nThe star is losing mass very fast. Huge bursts of gas, a bit like the outbursts on the Sun but far larger, have thrown out arcs, knots and jets. Together they built a big, lumpy cloud of gas and dust around it. That dust hides much of its visible light. So even though it is so luminous, you need a telescope to see it, and from Earth it ranges between magnitude 6.5 and 9.6.\n\nJérôme Lalande, a French astronomer, listed it in 1801 as a star of the 7th magnitude. Since 1847 people have called it a deep red, crimson star. In the 1800s observers thought they saw at least six stars there. These turned out to be bright patches in its cloud. It has grown dimmer as seen from Earth since 1850.\n\nThe star is younger than 10 million years, about 8 million by one model, yet it is near the end of its life. Big stars burn out fast. It may blow up as a supernova, some time in the coming 100,000 years, or its core may fall straight in on itself. Either way, it will most likely leave a black hole.", photos: [{"src": "photos/vycma.webp", "alt": "Hubble image of the lumpy cloud of gas and dust that VY Canis Majoris has thrown off, with the bright star near its centre.", "credit": "NASA, ESA / Hubble, processed by Judy Schmidt, CC BY 2.0", "after": 2}] },
  st218: { text: "Stephenson 2-18, a huge red star also known as Stephenson 2 DFK 1, lies in Scutum, the Shield. It is a red supergiant, and maybe an even rarer red hypergiant. It is about 19,000 light years away, near a star cluster called Stephenson 2, in one of the spiral arms of the Milky Way. Most astronomers now count it as a member of that cluster.\n\nFew known stars are bigger. A 2026 study gives it a radius of about 1,840 times the Sun’s, so its volume is about 6.26 billion times larger. If it took the Sun’s place, its surface would reach past Jupiter’s orbit and nearly to Saturn’s. It gives off about 321,000 times as much light as the Sun, yet its surface is cool for a star, about 3,200 K, which is why it glows red.\n\nIts size has been hard to pin down. A 2013 study made it even bigger, about 2,150 times the Sun’s radius, which is larger than theory says a star like this can be. A star that big could not stay steady. Because the distance may be wrong by up to half, the size is uncertain too.\n\nIt first entered a catalogue in 1988, as IRAS 18363-0607. In 1990 Charles Bruce Stephenson found the cluster in data from an infrared survey. The first guess put it about 98,000 light years away, far more than today’s value. For years astronomers argued about whether the star truly belongs to the cluster, since it moves a little differently from its neighbours.\n\nStephenson 2-18 is shedding its outer layers fast, about 1.35 hundred-thousandths of the Sun’s mass each year, one of the highest rates known for a red supergiant. It may be close to blowing off those layers and becoming a very hot star, such as a luminous blue variable or a Wolf-Rayet star.", photos: [{"src": "photos/st218.webp", "alt": "An infrared sky image with Stephenson 2-18 as a bright orange star in the centre and its cluster at upper left.", "credit": "2MASS, public domain", "after": 0}] },
  wohg64: { text: "WOH G64 is a giant star that belongs to the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy that travels around our own Milky Way. Its distance from us is about 163,000 light years. It does not live alone: it has a smaller, hotter partner, a star of type B.\n\nThe main star is a red supergiant, a cool and hugely swollen star, with a surface of only about 3,400 K. It could be the biggest star whose size has been measured well, at about 1,540 times the Sun’s radius. If it took the Sun’s place, its surface would reach beyond the orbit of Jupiter. Even light would need more than six hours to travel once around it. It has about 20 times the Sun’s mass and shines about 282,000 times as brightly.\n\nThe star is losing mass fast through a strong wind. This lost material has built a thick shell of dust about one light year across, as heavy as 3 to 9 Suns. In 2007 the Very Large Telescope showed that the dust is shaped like a ring or a doughnut. Astronomers call this shape a torus.\n\nBengt Westerlund, Nils Olander and B. Hedin found the star in the 1970s, and the letters WOH come from their surnames. In 2024 the VLTI, which combines the light of several large telescopes, made a close-up picture of the dust around it. It was the first picture of this kind of a star in another galaxy.\n\nSince 2016 the light of WOH G64 has shown signs of its partner, which pulls gas off the giant. Around 2014 the way its brightness changes became irregular. In 2024 one team said the star had turned into a yellow hypergiant about half its old size. A 2026 study disagreed. It found that the star remains a red supergiant, and that a close pass of the partner had only stretched its outer layers for a while.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wohg64.webp", "alt": "The VLTI close-up of WOH G64: a glowing oval blob, the star in its dust cocoon, inside a fainter loop of dust.", "credit": "ESO/K. Ohnaka et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 3}] },
  quasi: { text: "Nobody has found one yet; it is a model. In the young universe, the core of a giant protostar might have collapsed into a black hole while the outer layers stayed put, lit by gas pouring into the hole rather than by fusion. Such a star could swell to about 8,200 to 40,700 R☉ and rival a small galaxy in light. After a brief 7 to 10 million year life it would fade out, leaving a black hole seed for today’s giants." },
  wr140: { text: "WR 140 is a pair of very hot, heavy stars in the constellation Cygnus. It lies about 5,000 light years away, in the middle of the triangle made by Deneb, Gamma Cygni and Delta Cygni. One star is a Wolf–Rayet star, a massive star late in its life that is blowing off its outer layers. The other is a big, bright O-type star.\n\nThe two stars swing around each other every 7.9 years on a long, stretched orbit. At their closest they are 1.3 AU apart, a little more than the gap between Earth and the Sun. At their farthest they are 23.9 AU apart. The O star holds about 29 times the Sun’s mass and the Wolf–Rayet star about 10.\n\nBoth stars blow strong winds of gas. Shortly after the stars pass closest, the system makes a fresh burst of dust rich in carbon, a kind of soot, and grows much brighter in infrared light. This happens about every eight years. New bursts were seen in 1985, 1993, 2001 and 2009. Each burst adds a new shell, so the stars sit inside a set of rings, one inside the next.\n\nThe first shells were seen from the Keck Observatory around 1999 to 2001, but telescopes on the ground could only pick out one or two. In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope saw more than 17 shells, reaching about 70,000 AU from the stars. Together they record over 130 years of dust. The dust leaves at about 1,800 km each second and keeps speeding up, pushed by the light of the stars.\n\nWR 140 is the classic example of a colliding-wind binary, a pair whose winds crash together, and of how stars spread dust into space. Most Wolf–Rayet stars wrap their dust in spirals, so these neat rings are odd. No one is yet sure why they are so regular.", photos: [{"src": "photos/wr140.webp", "alt": "James Webb Space Telescope infrared image of WR 140, a bright star with spikes, wrapped in many thin, nested rings of dust.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / JWST MIRI, Ryan Lau et al., processed by Meli thev, CC BY-SA 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  vfts352: { text: "VFTS 352 is a pair of stars in the Tarantula Nebula, a huge cloud where new stars form. The nebula lies inside the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy close to our own, some 160,000 light years from us. In our sky it is in the southern constellation Dorado.\n\nThe two stars sit so close together that their surfaces touch and their outer layers overlap. Astronomers call this a contact binary. No heavier pair of this kind is known. Each star has about 29 times the Sun’s mass and about 7 times its radius. They swing around each other in about 27 hours. Each star also turns once per orbit, so they always show each other the same face.\n\nBoth are O-type stars, the hottest and heaviest kind of ordinary star. Their surfaces are above 40,000 K, and each shines 150,000 to 180,000 times as brightly as the Sun. They are very young, only about 1 million years old.\n\nThe pair was found with the Very Large Telescope of ESO, the European Southern Observatory, and the team made it public on 13 October 2015. Massive stars like these matter to us. They make most of the oxygen in the universe deep inside them, and when they explode as supernovae they scatter it into space.\n\nNo one knows how the story will end, but there are two likely paths. The stars may merge into one star that spins very fast. Such a star might die in a long burst of gamma rays, the most energetic kind of light. Or each star may explode as a supernova and leave a black hole behind. The two black holes would then circle each other closely, and if they later merge, they would send out gravitational waves, ripples in space itself.", photos: [{"src": "photos/vfts352.webp", "alt": "A telescope image of the Tarantula Nebula, with red crosshairs marking VFTS 352 at the centre.", "credit": "ESO/M.-R. Cioni/VISTA Magellanic Cloud survey, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}] },
  xxtri: { text: "XX Trianguli is a variable star, which means a star whose brightness changes. It lies in Triangulum, a small northern constellation, close to where it meets Andromeda. It is about 640 light years away and is slowly coming closer to us, at about 26 km each second. Its magnitude moves between 8.1 and 8.7, so it is too dim to see with the eye alone.\n\nThe star we see is an orange giant. It has used up the hydrogen in its core, so it has cooled and swelled to about 11 times the Sun’s width. Its mass is about 1.26 times the Sun’s, and it gives out about 30 times as much light. Its surface is about 4,620 K, and its age is about eight billion years.\n\nWhat makes XX Trianguli famous is its spots. It has dark patches on its surface, called starspots, and one of them is wider than the whole Sun. Astronomers found it with Doppler imaging, a method that tracks small shifts in the star’s light as it spins. Most of its spots sit near the poles and at high latitudes, with now and then a few small ones near the middle.\n\nIt spins once in about 24 days, which is quick for a star this big. Its middle and its poles turn at slightly different speeds, much as on the Sun, but the difference is small. Its magnetic activity also seems to rise and fall in a cycle of about 26 years. Only one such cycle has been watched so far, so this is not yet sure.\n\nThe star also has a partner that nobody can see. We know it is there only because the giant’s light shifts back and forth as the pair goes around each other every 24 days. Brian Skiff, of Lowell Observatory, probably first noticed the star’s changing brightness in 1985. It got its variable star name, XX Trianguli, in 1993.", photos: [{"src": "photos/xxtri.webp", "alt": "Four Doppler maps of XX Trianguli (HD 12545) seen from different sides, with a huge dark starspot over the pole and the Sun drawn small for scale.", "credit": "K. Strassmeier / NOAO/AURA/NSF, CC BY 4.0", "after": 2}] },
  epsaur: { text: "Roughly every 27 years this yellow supergiant fades for 640 to 730 days as something vast and almost dark passes in front of it. Astronomers once blamed a giant see-through star or even a black hole. The eclipser now looks like a dusty disk about 3.8 AU wide, wrapped around a hidden star or pair of stars, that blocks some 70% of the light. During the 2009 to 2011 eclipse, the CHARA array imaged its silhouette." },
  mothra: { text: "Mothra is a pair of giant stars in a faraway galaxy, seen in the direction of the constellation Eridanus. It is some 10.5 billion light years from us. Few star systems this far away have ever been found.\n\nA star so far away should be far too faint to see on its own. We can see Mothra because of gravitational lensing: the gravity of a heavy object in front of it bends and boosts its light, like a giant magnifying glass. Its home galaxy lies behind a huge cluster of galaxies called MACS J0416.1−2403, which has the nickname Christmas Tree Galaxy Cluster.\n\nThe extra boost for Mothra comes from a small unseen object very close to our line of sight, called a millilens. It has between 10,000 and a million times the Sun’s mass. It may be a small globular cluster, a tight ball of old stars, or a clump of dark matter, matter that we cannot see. It is one of the smallest lumps of matter ever found this far away by lensing.\n\nMothra is two stars. The larger is a yellow supergiant or hypergiant with a radius of about 271 R☉ and a surface of about 5,250 K. It began life with about 15 times the Sun’s mass. Its partner is a blue supergiant about 60 R☉ in radius and about 14,000 K. The blue star is the brighter of the two, at about 125,000 times the Sun’s light, against 50,000 for the yellow one.\n\nThe Spanish astrophysicist Jose Diego gave it the nickname Mothra, a nod to how monstrous it is. It also goes by LS1, short for lensed star 1. Its brightness may change over time. As of 2026, no one knows how the two stars move around each other.", photos: [{"src": "photos/mothra.webp", "alt": "Image of the galaxy cluster MACS0416 with a close-up box on the stretched, lensed background galaxy that holds Mothra.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA, STScI, J. Diego et al., CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}] },
  earendel: { text: "Earendel, also called WHL0137-LS, is a very faint, very distant object in Cetus, the Whale. If it is just one star, it is the farthest star ever seen. Its light set out when the universe was only about 900 million years old, and travelled for 12.9 billion years to reach us. Because space has kept stretching, the place where it is now lies about 28 billion light years from us.\n\nWe can only see it thanks to a lucky line-up. A huge group of galaxies, WHL0137-08, sits between it and us. Its gravity bends and focuses Earendel’s light like a giant lens. Early studies said this made Earendel brighter by a factor of 1,000 to 40,000. The galaxy it lives in was stretched into a long curve called the Sunrise Arc.\n\nHubble found Earendel, and the news was shared with the world on 30 March 2022. In Old English the name means the \"morning star\", also read as \"rising light\". It also nods to Eärendil, a sky-sailing character in Tolkien’s The Silmarillion. Webb took its first picture of it on 30 July 2022.\n\nIn 2023 Webb data pointed to a heavy, blue-white star of type B, with a surface over two times the Sun’s temperature and a glow roughly a million times stronger. Webb also saw signs of a cooler, red companion. As one lone star it would be 13,000 to 16,000 K and might hold 20 to 200 times the Sun’s mass.\n\nNewer work in 2025 put the lensing boost much lower, only about 43 to 67 times. Webb’s spectra now hint that Earendel is probably a cluster of many stars, perhaps a young globular cluster poor in heavy elements. A single star has not been ruled out, so its true nature is still open.", photos: [{"src": "photos/earendel.webp", "alt": "A Webb image of the galaxy cluster WHL0137-08, with an inset of the stretched Sunrise Arc galaxy where Earendel sits.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, CSA / Webb, D. Coe, Z. Levay, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}] },
  powehi: { text: "Pōwehi is the giant black hole at the heart of Messier 87, a huge galaxy in Virgo. Its catalogue name is M87*. The galaxy lies about 53 million light years away and holds several trillion stars. A black hole is a place where matter is packed so tightly that nothing, not even light, can get out.\n\nPōwehi is one of the heaviest black holes known. Measurements from 2019 put its mass at about 6.5 billion times the Sun’s. The edge of no return lies about 120 AU from its centre, about three times as far as Pluto is from the Sun. A disk of hot gas swirls around it at up to about 1,000 km each second. It swallows about one Sun’s worth of gas every ten years.\n\nPōwehi also shoots out a narrow jet of hot, electrically charged gas at nearly the speed of light. The jet reaches at least 4,900 light years out from the middle of the galaxy. Heber Curtis noticed it in 1918 and called it a curious straight ray.\n\nCharles Messier listed the galaxy in 1781, taking it for a faint cloud. In 2017 the Event Horizon Telescope, a set of radio telescopes around the world working as one, watched the black hole. The team showed the result on 10 April 2019. It was the first picture ever made of a black hole, with a dark shadow inside an uneven ring of glowing gas about 690 AU wide.\n\nAfter the picture came out, the black hole got the name Pōwehi. It comes from the Kumulipo, an old Hawaiian creation chant, and it speaks of a dark, endless, adorned creation. In 2021 the team showed how the ring looks in polarised light. That view reveals the magnetic fields close to Pōwehi, and these may help explain how it drives its jet.", photos: [{"src": "photos/powehi.webp", "alt": "The Event Horizon Telescope picture of Pōwehi: a dark shadow inside a lopsided ring of glowing orange gas.", "credit": "Event Horizon Telescope / ESO, CC BY 4.0", "after": 0}] },
  wr124: { text: "Few stars in our galaxy flee faster: this hot Wolf-Rayet star in Sagitta moves at about 200 km/s. Its fierce wind has thrown off a glowing shell, the nebula M1-67, almost 6 ly wide and growing at more than 150,000 km/h. Single clumps in that shell carry up to 30 Earth masses and would stretch as far as Saturn lies from the Sun. Within some hundreds of thousands of years, the star itself should explode as a supernova." },
  siriusb: { text: "Sirius B is a white dwarf, the small, faint partner of Sirius, the brightest star we see at night. The pair lies about 8.6 light years away in Canis Major, the Greater Dog. In visible light Sirius B is about 10,000 times fainter than its partner Sirius A, so it hides in the glare of its bright companion.\n\nA white dwarf is the hot, leftover core of a star that has used up its fuel. Sirius B packs about 1.02 times the Sun’s mass into a ball close to the size of Earth, about 12,000 km across. That makes it one of the heaviest white dwarfs known, nearly twice the usual mass. Its surface is about 25,200 K. Its outer layer is almost pure hydrogen.\n\nThe two stars circle each other every 50 years. The gap between them changes from 8.2 to 31.5 AU, about 20 AU on average, roughly as far as Uranus is from the Sun. In X-rays the roles swap, and Sirius B outshines Sirius A.\n\nIn 1844 Friedrich Bessel, a German astronomer, saw Sirius wobble as it moved and worked out that it must have an unseen partner. On 31 January 1862 Alvan Graham Clark first saw it while testing a new telescope with a 470 mm lens. In 1915 Walter Adams studied its light and found a faint whitish star. It was only the second white dwarf ever found.\n\nLong ago Sirius B was the bigger star of the pair. It began as a blue B-type star of about 5 times the Sun’s mass. It burned through its hydrogen, grew into a red giant, then lost its outer layers and shrank to a white dwarf. That was some 120 million years in the past. With no fuel left to burn, it will keep cooling for about two billion more years.", photos: [{"src": "photos/siriusb.webp", "alt": "Hubble image of the glaring, overexposed Sirius A with spikes and rings, and Sirius B as a tiny dot to its lower left.", "credit": "NASA, ESA, H. Bond and M. Barstow / Hubble, public domain", "after": 0}] },
  neutron: { text: "The Vela pulsar is what remains of a star that exploded: its tiny, fast-spinning core. It lies in Vela, the Sails, inside the glowing wreck of that explosion, the Vela supernova remnant. It is roughly 1,000 light years from Earth.\n\nA pulsar is a neutron star, a ball of matter packed so tightly that about 1.4 times the Sun’s mass fits into a space smaller than a city. As it spins, beams from its poles sweep past Earth like a lighthouse, so we see pulses. The Vela pulsar turns about 11 times every second, once every 89 thousandths of a second. In radio waves it is the brightest pulsar in the sky, and it is the brightest steady source of high-energy gamma rays. In visible light it is very faint, at magnitude 23.6.\n\nThe star blew up as a Type II supernova about 11,000 to 12,300 years ago. In 1968 a team from the University of Sydney found that the pulsar belongs to the remnant. This was the first direct proof that supernovae leave neutron stars behind. At the time its spin was the fastest known for a pulsar.\n\nVela is famous for glitches, sudden small jumps in its spin rate. They come about every three years on average, and no one can yet predict them. On 12 December 2016 a radio telescope able to see single pulses watched one happen live, a first. During the glitch the pulsar skipped one pulse, and the whole jump took under five seconds. Another came on 22 July 2021.\n\nThe pulsar powers a cloud of fast particles around it, and jets that shoot out at about 70 percent of the speed of light. Its signals even became music: the composer Gérard Grisey used them in Le noir de l’étoile.", photos: [{"src": "photos/neutron.webp", "alt": "A Chandra X-ray image of the Vela pulsar inside its glowing cloud of particles, with a jet streaming from it.", "credit": "NASA/CXC/PSU/G. Pavlov et al. / Chandra, public domain", "after": 1}] },
  tzo: { text: "A Thorne–Żytkow object is a kind of star that scientists think could exist, but none has been found for certain. Seen from outside, it would look like a red giant or a red supergiant, a huge swollen star. Hidden at its centre would be a neutron star, the tiny, dense leftover of a big star that has exploded.\n\nKip Thorne and Anna Żytkow first put forward the idea in 1977. Such an object could form when a neutron star crashes into a giant star. This could happen in a crowded star cluster, or in a pair of stars after one of them explodes as a supernova: the blast can knock the neutron star onto a path that runs into its partner, or the partner can later swell up and swallow it.\n\nDeep inside, the buried neutron star would have a surface hotter than a billion K, more than nearly any star’s core. Gas falling onto it could fuse in unusual ways and make odd mixes of elements, such as extra rubidium, lithium or molybdenum, that could give the object away.\n\nIn 2014 Emily Levesque and her team said that HV 2112, a star of the Small Magellanic Cloud, might be the first one found. In 2018 a group led by Emma Beasor argued against this and named a different star, HV 11417, as a candidate instead. Neither is confirmed. A sure answer may need both the light of the star and the gravitational waves, tiny ripples in space, sent out by the neutron star inside.\n\nSuch an object would not last long, only about 100,000 to a million years. Even so, there could be 20 to 200 of them in the Milky Way today. In the end the star may lose its outer layers, leaving a neutron star ringed by a large disk of gas. Or the neutron star may swallow enough gas to turn into a black hole." },
  cygx1: { text: "Spotted by an X-ray counter on a rocket in 1964, it became the first X-ray source widely accepted as a black hole. Estimates put its mass between about 14 and 21 M☉; every 5.6 days it circles a blue supergiant, feeding on the star’s wind and tugging it into a teardrop. Stephen Hawking bet Kip Thorne it was no black hole, a wager he wanted to lose, and conceded in 1990." },
  sgra: { text: "Sagittarius A*, a supermassive black hole millions of times heavier than the Sun, sits at the centre of the Milky Way, our galaxy. From Earth it lies near the border of Sagittarius and Scorpius, about 27,000 light years away. Its name is said as Sagittarius A-star.\n\nIt has about 4.3 million times the Sun’s mass. A black hole’s gravity is so strong that not even light can get out. So we cannot see the black hole itself, only gas, dust and stars near it. The radio and infrared glow we pick up comes from gas that grows millions of degrees hot as it falls inward. Thick dust between us and the centre of the galaxy blocks all of its visible light.\n\nIn May 2022 the Event Horizon Telescope, eight radio observatories spread across the world and linked together, released the first picture of the glowing ring around it. It was the second black hole ever imaged, after the one in the galaxy M87. The ring is about 64 million km across, about 46 times as wide as the Sun.\n\nBruce Balick and Robert Brown found it on 13 and 15 February 1974 as a small, bright radio source. Brown added the star symbol to its name in 1982, because the source was \"exciting\", and in physics excited atoms are marked with an asterisk.\n\nFor decades, teams tracked stars racing around it. One star, S2, reached 7,650 km each second on its closest pass in 2018. From these orbits they showed that only a black hole could hold so much mass in so small a space. This work won Reinhard Genzel and Andrea Ghez a share of the 2020 Nobel Prize in Physics. The black hole sometimes flares. In 2019 it suddenly shone 75 times more brightly than normal.", photos: [{"src": "photos/sgra.webp", "alt": "Event Horizon Telescope radio image of Sagittarius A*: a glowing orange ring around a dark centre.", "credit": "EHT Collaboration / ESO, CC BY 4.0", "after": 1}] },
  qso: { text: "Quasar 3C 273 lies in Virgo, the Maiden. A quasar is the blazing centre of a galaxy, powered by a giant black hole. This one is about 2.4 billion light years away, at the heart of a huge, egg-shaped galaxy. No quasar was identified before it, and none in our sky is brighter: it reaches magnitude 12.9. With a 6-inch telescope, amateurs can see it, which makes it the most distant thing most of them will ever spot.\n\nIts power is hard to imagine. In visible light it shines more than 4 trillion times as brightly as the Sun, and more than 16 times as brightly as its whole home galaxy. The energy comes from gas falling onto a supermassive black hole hundreds of millions of times the Sun’s mass. Its brightness changes over days to decades.\n\nA jet of particles moving near the speed of light shoots out from it. The jet is about 200,000 light years long. In 1995 the Hubble Space Telescope showed it is made of bright knots with dimmer gaps between them.\n\nThe name means it was object number 273 in the Third Cambridge Catalogue of radio sources, published in 1959. Cyril Hazard used the Moon passing in front of it to fix its position, and a starlike point of light was found there. Its spectrum looked like no known star. Maarten Schmidt and Bev Oke showed in 1963 that its light is stretched by a redshift of 0.158, so it must be billions of light years away. That made it the first known quasar.\n\nPartly because it is so bright in radio waves, its position helps define the system astronomers use to map the sky. It can be seen from March to July from both halves of the Earth.", photos: [{"src": "photos/qso.webp", "alt": "A Hubble image of the quasar 3C 273 as a brilliant point of light.", "credit": "ESA/Hubble & NASA, CC BY 2.0", "after": 0}] },
};
